WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.850
Good docking is nearly all planning, and very little of it is hand skill.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.900
Approach slowly.

3
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:11.900
The old rule is: never approach a dock faster than you’re willing to hit it.

4
00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:16.625
Work out which is stronger, the wind or the current, and approach into it.

5
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:18.525
Why into it?

6
00:00:19.525 --> 00:00:21.675
Because it acts as your brake.

7
00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:25.350
You keep steerage, and you can stop whenever you like.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.625
Have your fenders and lines rigged before you arrive, not while you’re drifting down on the pilings.

9
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:33.750
Use a spring line.

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.900
One spring made fast will hold the boat against the dock while you get the others on, or pivot her in or out.

11
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.950
And brief your crew and your passengers.

12
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.850
Fend off with a fender or a boat hook.

13
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.300
Never with hands or feet.

14
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.025
A boat weighs tons, and it doesn’t stop for a foot.
